WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.281 --> 00:00:02.562
- The only thing I have
I ever wanted to be

00:00:02.562 --> 00:00:03.817
for my whole life was a writer.

00:00:03.817 --> 00:00:06.357
Creative living is any life that you live

00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:08.258
where your decisions
are based more strongly

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:10.104
on your curiosity than your fear.

00:00:10.104 --> 00:00:12.187
The definition of your
ego is it's a bottomless

00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:14.858
hole of desire, it can never
be satisfied with anything.

00:00:14.858 --> 00:00:16.262
You can't open a magazine today,

00:00:16.262 --> 00:00:18.090
without somebody, the headline is always,

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.489
how so and so found balance!

00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:22.056
For me, in my life, writing
is the thing that I love.

00:00:22.056 --> 00:00:23.943
And the (bleep) sandwich
was the seven years

00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:25.713
that I was not getting published.

00:00:25.713 --> 00:00:28.664
With creativity, even my failures,

00:00:28.664 --> 00:00:30.393
the stakes are so low.

00:00:30.393 --> 00:00:32.479
Aside from people who are
very heavily medicated,

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:34.149
the calmest people that I know

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:36.037
tend to also be the wisest.

00:00:36.037 --> 00:00:37.796
If you can't feel like you're alive

00:00:37.796 --> 00:00:41.065
unless you're burning bridges behind you,

00:00:41.065 --> 00:00:43.226
light the match, walk
away, that's your deal.

00:00:43.226 --> 00:00:47.574
I'm operating constantly
at like 90 percent

00:00:47.574 --> 00:00:50.320
fear threshold, I'm afraid right now.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.607
- She's an American author, essayist,

00:00:52.607 --> 00:00:54.410
biographer, and novelist.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.719
She's best known for her book,

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:58.534
Eat Pray Love, which has spent 199 weeks

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:01.208
on the New York Times bestseller list.

00:01:01.208 --> 00:01:03.409
Eat Pray Love was also turned into a film

00:01:03.409 --> 00:01:05.981
by the same name in 2010.

00:01:05.981 --> 00:01:07.752
She's Elizabeth Gilbert,
and here's my take

00:01:07.752 --> 00:01:10.419
on her top 10 rules for success.

00:01:13.282 --> 00:01:15.610
- The only thing I have ever wanted to be

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.021
for my whole life was a writer.

00:01:17.021 --> 00:01:19.859
I wrote all through childhood,
all through adolescence,

00:01:19.859 --> 00:01:21.321
by the time I was a
teenager I was sending my

00:01:21.321 --> 00:01:22.911
very bad stories to the New Yorker,

00:01:22.911 --> 00:01:24.148
hoping to be discovered,

00:01:24.148 --> 00:01:26.591
after college I got a
job as a diner waitress,

00:01:26.591 --> 00:01:30.011
kept working, kept writing,
kept trying really hard

00:01:30.011 --> 00:01:32.362
to get published, and failing at it.

00:01:32.362 --> 00:01:35.942
I failed at getting published
for almost six years.

00:01:35.942 --> 00:01:37.612
So for almost six years, every single day

00:01:37.612 --> 00:01:39.311
I had nothing but rejection
letters waiting for me

00:01:39.311 --> 00:01:41.716
in my mailbox and it was devastating

00:01:41.716 --> 00:01:43.394
every single time and every single time

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:45.960
I had to ask myself if I should just quit

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.830
while I was behind, and give up,

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.080
and spare myself this pain,

00:01:50.916 --> 00:01:52.143
but then I would find my resolve

00:01:52.143 --> 00:01:54.313
and always in the same way, by saying,

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:57.571
I'm not going to quit, I'm going home.

00:01:57.571 --> 00:01:58.738
You have to understand that for me,

00:01:58.738 --> 00:02:00.746
going home did not mean returning

00:02:00.746 --> 00:02:02.068
to my family's farm.

00:02:02.068 --> 00:02:04.758
For me, going home meant
returning to the work

00:02:04.758 --> 00:02:07.045
of writing because writing was my home.

00:02:07.045 --> 00:02:09.469
Because I loved writing
more than I hated failing

00:02:09.469 --> 00:02:11.706
at writing, which is to
say that I loved writing

00:02:11.706 --> 00:02:13.492
more than I loved my own ego,

00:02:13.492 --> 00:02:14.845
which is ultimately to say,

00:02:14.845 --> 00:02:17.447
that I loved writing
more than I loved myself.

00:02:17.447 --> 00:02:18.740
And that's how I pushed through it.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.611
My definition, personally of creativity,

00:02:20.611 --> 00:02:24.519
is that it's the intersection
between a human's labor

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:26.251
and the mysteries of inspiration.

00:02:26.251 --> 00:02:27.459
Which is kind of grand.

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:30.426
But my definition of creative
living is much simpler.

00:02:30.426 --> 00:02:33.276
Creative living is any life that you live

00:02:33.276 --> 00:02:35.232
where your decisions
are based more strongly

00:02:35.232 --> 00:02:37.107
on your curiosity than your fear.

00:02:37.107 --> 00:02:38.251
That's it.

00:02:38.251 --> 00:02:41.036
And if you consistently,
habitually, routinely,

00:02:41.036 --> 00:02:44.275
at every intersection and
decision in your life,

00:02:44.275 --> 00:02:47.416
make decisions that are based
on curiosity rather than fear,

00:02:47.416 --> 00:02:49.623
then you will be engaging with creativity

00:02:49.623 --> 00:02:50.632
at some level, at some point.

00:02:50.632 --> 00:02:52.594
And it won't look like my life,

00:02:52.594 --> 00:02:54.197
it will look like your life,

00:02:54.197 --> 00:02:56.206
it won't look like anybody else's life.

00:02:56.206 --> 00:02:58.695
Because everybody's
curiosity is different,

00:02:58.695 --> 00:03:00.658
so everybody's path of following curiosity

00:03:00.658 --> 00:03:02.224
will have a different outcome,

00:03:02.224 --> 00:03:05.802
but your life itself
will become a work of art

00:03:05.802 --> 00:03:08.681
if you can consistently live that way.

00:03:08.681 --> 00:03:12.691
Not easy, but so worth
it, so worth the trouble.

00:03:12.691 --> 00:03:14.004
(laughs)

00:03:14.004 --> 00:03:16.433
It's perfectly okay to have an ego,

00:03:16.433 --> 00:03:18.774
we all have one, the
definition of your ego

00:03:18.774 --> 00:03:20.733
is it's a bottomless hole of desire,

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:22.636
it can never be satisfied with anything.

00:03:22.636 --> 00:03:25.027
Your ego is the thing that
will sell 20 million records

00:03:25.027 --> 00:03:28.610
and wonder why it wasn't
30 million, right?

00:03:30.207 --> 00:03:33.044
And it can be satisfied for moments,

00:03:33.044 --> 00:03:34.477
but it can't be eternally satisfied.

00:03:34.477 --> 00:03:35.670
No matter what your ego gets,

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:36.866
there's going to be another level

00:03:36.866 --> 00:03:38.388
where it wants more,
it wants five of them,

00:03:38.388 --> 00:03:41.965
it wants them in blue, it
wants it tomorrow morning.

00:03:41.965 --> 00:03:43.933
We all have this, at
some level in ourselves.

00:03:43.933 --> 00:03:46.423
So I had a letter on
Facebook the other day

00:03:46.423 --> 00:03:47.892
from a young woman who said,

00:03:47.892 --> 00:03:50.075
I don't think it's fair
for anybody to tell me

00:03:50.075 --> 00:03:52.935
that I shouldn't want to
be a successful actress.

00:03:52.935 --> 00:03:54.241
I'm going to acting school and I want

00:03:54.241 --> 00:03:55.530
to be a successful actress.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.680
And I said of course you want
to be a successful actress,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.738
I wanted to be a successful writer.

00:03:59.738 --> 00:04:01.637
When I was your age I
wanted to be published,

00:04:01.637 --> 00:04:03.550
if I didn't want to be published
I wouldn't have bothered

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.102
trying to get published,
I would've just written

00:04:05.102 --> 00:04:06.914
in my bedroom and put it under my desk

00:04:06.914 --> 00:04:10.263
and been fine but I wanted
it to be seen, it's fine.

00:04:10.263 --> 00:04:13.780
But, make sure you have,
if you're not capable

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.292
of having a stronger motive than that,

00:04:16.292 --> 00:04:19.148
make sure you at least
have an additional one.

00:04:19.148 --> 00:04:21.223
Make sure you have an additional one.

00:04:21.223 --> 00:04:23.580
And let that additional one be something

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.008
that has more to do with
your soul than your ego.

00:04:26.008 --> 00:04:28.115
Because you are also a soul.

00:04:28.115 --> 00:04:29.678
And your soul wants a
bunch of different stuff

00:04:29.678 --> 00:04:30.816
than your ego wants.

00:04:30.816 --> 00:04:32.556
Your soul wants wonder, it wants becoming,

00:04:32.556 --> 00:04:35.911
it wants unfolding, it wants
process, it wants connection,

00:04:35.911 --> 00:04:37.979
it wants some sort of sense of meaning,

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:39.732
those are the things your soul wants.

00:04:39.732 --> 00:04:42.472
And I have this lecture
with myself all the time,

00:04:42.472 --> 00:04:45.137
so that when I get a bad
review and I'm miffed about it

00:04:45.137 --> 00:04:46.599
and I feel like I've been misunderstood,

00:04:46.599 --> 00:04:47.737
or worse, understood,

00:04:47.737 --> 00:04:49.518
(laughs)

00:04:49.518 --> 00:04:51.855
and revealed in a way
that I don't want to be,

00:04:51.855 --> 00:04:53.543
I can see this difference
where I can check

00:04:53.543 --> 00:04:55.350
in with my ego and my ego's wounded,

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.687
it doesn't like it, I would like everybody

00:04:57.687 --> 00:05:00.174
to like everything I do, it hurts.

00:05:00.174 --> 00:05:02.343
I still don't like it,
after all these years.

00:05:02.343 --> 00:05:05.782
And I'll be like ouch,
that didn't feel good.

00:05:05.782 --> 00:05:06.635
I didn't want that.

00:05:06.635 --> 00:05:09.038
And then I check in with my soul,

00:05:09.038 --> 00:05:10.011
and say, how you doing?

00:05:10.011 --> 00:05:12.932
And my soul's like, we're
fine, can we play more?

00:05:12.932 --> 00:05:13.967
Can we make another thing?

00:05:13.967 --> 00:05:15.404
I just want to make another thing.

00:05:15.404 --> 00:05:17.717
Let's do it again, let's do another one.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:21.014
Let's do it differently, let's try this.

00:05:21.014 --> 00:05:22.782
I can never totally circumvent my ego

00:05:22.782 --> 00:05:25.713
but as long as I'm
making sure to constantly

00:05:25.713 --> 00:05:28.983
check in with a richer and more
interesting part of myself,

00:05:28.983 --> 00:05:30.853
then I feel like I'm alright.

00:05:30.853 --> 00:05:33.013
- And let's throw out that word balance,

00:05:33.013 --> 00:05:36.210
we're always trying to say
balance, and what is that?

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.006
- That is a word I would
like to see eradicated,

00:05:39.006 --> 00:05:39.839
(laughs)

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.160
from the cultural conversation,
I mean you can't open

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.238
a magazine today without somebody,

00:05:43.238 --> 00:05:45.212
the headline is always, how
so and so found balance,

00:05:45.212 --> 00:05:46.762
how this person found balance,

00:05:46.762 --> 00:05:49.200
have you ever met that
person, first of all,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.002
and if you did, would you
want to even be friends

00:05:52.002 --> 00:05:53.371
with them, is that somebody
who you'd want to call

00:05:53.371 --> 00:05:54.970
in the middle of the
night when you've lost

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.089
your balance, balance is
something that you find

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.603
and lose, regain and
lose, we're on a planet

00:05:59.603 --> 00:06:01.698
that's spinning 7,000 miles an hour,

00:06:01.698 --> 00:06:03.791
our minds are spinning
20,000 miles an hour,

00:06:03.791 --> 00:06:05.431
we're surrounded by
people who are in change,

00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:07.176
we're in change, to expect that you should

00:06:07.176 --> 00:06:09.134
somehow be able to hold your state

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:11.079
through that entire thing is just crazy!

00:06:11.079 --> 00:06:13.615
Every pursuit, no matter
how glamorous it may seem,

00:06:13.615 --> 00:06:16.116
no matter how exciting it feels to you,

00:06:16.116 --> 00:06:18.473
no matter how much you feel
you were born to do it,

00:06:18.473 --> 00:06:19.993
comes with a (bleep) sandwich.

00:06:19.993 --> 00:06:22.927
So the question is not, what do I love?

00:06:22.927 --> 00:06:25.493
The question is what do I love so much

00:06:25.493 --> 00:06:27.148
that I don't mind eating
the (bleep) sandwich

00:06:27.148 --> 00:06:28.753
that comes along with that thing?

00:06:28.753 --> 00:06:31.932
So for me, in my life, writing
is the thing that I love.

00:06:31.932 --> 00:06:33.762
And the (bleep) sandwich
was the seven years

00:06:33.762 --> 00:06:35.307
that I was not getting published.

00:06:35.307 --> 00:06:38.499
And that I was coming home from
my job as a diner waitress,

00:06:38.499 --> 00:06:41.402
as a bartender, as an au
pair, as somebody who worked

00:06:41.402 --> 00:06:43.740
at flea markets, as a
cook, and I was coming home

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.341
tired and smelling like
other people's french fries,

00:06:46.341 --> 00:06:48.322
and sitting down and doing my real job,

00:06:48.322 --> 00:06:51.076
which was to write, and
then to go to the mailbox

00:06:51.076 --> 00:06:53.851
the next day and get
another rejection letter.

00:06:53.851 --> 00:06:55.473
And then say, do I still want to do this?

00:06:55.473 --> 00:06:58.357
Cause this (bleep) sandwich sucks!

00:06:58.357 --> 00:06:59.668
(laughs)

00:06:59.668 --> 00:07:01.029
- Am I ready to take another bite?

00:07:01.029 --> 00:07:03.165
- And I did still want to do it.

00:07:03.165 --> 00:07:05.098
And now, even as somebody
who makes their living

00:07:05.098 --> 00:07:06.868
as a writer, there's no end
to the (bleep) sandwiches.

00:07:06.868 --> 00:07:10.631
It's like, oh hello,
horrible review in prominent

00:07:10.631 --> 00:07:13.922
newspaper, that's your
(bleep) sandwich today Liz,

00:07:13.922 --> 00:07:15.176
you still want to do this work?

00:07:15.176 --> 00:07:16.494
- [Interviewer] Yup.
- Yeah, I still do.

00:07:16.494 --> 00:07:17.636
Still worth it.

00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:20.459
Like, hello, awful comment on social media

00:07:20.459 --> 00:07:24.626
from somebody who thinks
you're a pile of dog (bleep)

00:07:25.647 --> 00:07:27.751
and has chosen every
possible way that they can,

00:07:27.751 --> 00:07:30.237
just cannot get it out of
their system fast enough,

00:07:30.237 --> 00:07:33.109
how much disregard they
have for your entire life,

00:07:33.109 --> 00:07:34.855
still want to do this thing?

00:07:34.855 --> 00:07:35.748
Yeah I still want to do it.

00:07:35.748 --> 00:07:38.279
So that's the question,
because the first time

00:07:38.279 --> 00:07:39.853
you encounter the (bleep)
sandwich, you're like,

00:07:39.853 --> 00:07:41.936
well this isn't worth it.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:44.990
Then that's not the thing
you're supposed to be doing.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.492
And there are plenty of
things in life that I have

00:07:47.492 --> 00:07:49.338
run into the (bleep)
sandwich and I've been like,

00:07:49.338 --> 00:07:53.505
so not worth it, so not worth
it, I don't want to do this.

00:07:54.777 --> 00:07:57.523
Like whatever the alleged
benefits of this thing might be,

00:07:57.523 --> 00:07:59.356
no, the Soulcycle, no!

00:08:00.538 --> 00:08:01.748
(laughs)

00:08:01.748 --> 00:08:04.093
Like, I know it's probably
really good for me,

00:08:04.093 --> 00:08:05.357
but I feel like throwing up right now

00:08:05.357 --> 00:08:06.549
because this is too hard,

00:08:06.549 --> 00:08:09.428
you'll not be seeing me here again.

00:08:09.428 --> 00:08:11.453
And I love my sisters at Soulcycle,

00:08:11.453 --> 00:08:12.984
I just don't like feeling
like I'm going to throw up

00:08:12.984 --> 00:08:16.780
when I exercise, so it's not for me.

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.392
And that's the question.

00:08:18.392 --> 00:08:20.337
So if you go into this thing thinking,

00:08:20.337 --> 00:08:23.200
if I follow my bliss, and I live my dream,

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.532
and I stand in my truth, then
everything will be great,

00:08:26.532 --> 00:08:29.078
it doesn't mean everything will be great.

00:08:29.078 --> 00:08:31.188
It just means at the end of
the day when you check in

00:08:31.188 --> 00:08:34.031
with yourself and you go, in the end,

00:08:34.031 --> 00:08:38.106
on the balance, is this still
better than not doing it,

00:08:38.106 --> 00:08:39.895
and the answer is still yeah,
this thing is still better

00:08:39.895 --> 00:08:42.312
than not doing it, then
you're on the right path.

00:08:42.312 --> 00:08:45.100
With creativity, even my failures,

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:48.047
the stakes are so low, you know,

00:08:48.047 --> 00:08:49.910
this is the thing that I'm
always trying to tell people

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:53.827
about artistic endeavor,
the stakes are so low,

00:08:55.485 --> 00:08:57.303
the stakes are so low,

00:08:57.303 --> 00:09:00.099
like very rarely is somebody going to die,

00:09:00.099 --> 00:09:03.325
or have their heart broken,
or have their life upturned

00:09:03.325 --> 00:09:06.653
because a poem that you wrote didn't work,

00:09:06.653 --> 00:09:09.386
or an essay that you tried
to write didn't work.

00:09:09.386 --> 00:09:11.871
If I write a book and
people don't like it,

00:09:11.871 --> 00:09:14.449
they're not going to come
to my house and shoot me.

00:09:14.449 --> 00:09:17.497
I'm exaggerating but I think sometimes we,

00:09:17.497 --> 00:09:21.107
in the realm of creativity,
blow the stakes up

00:09:21.107 --> 00:09:23.226
to something so much higher
than they possibly are.

00:09:23.226 --> 00:09:24.942
I've heard a lot of comics say

00:09:24.942 --> 00:09:27.491
that the fantastic thing
about doing stand-up

00:09:27.491 --> 00:09:30.549
which I think is the
scariest of all art forms

00:09:30.549 --> 00:09:33.549
is to bomb and then to walk offstage

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:38.630
and you're like, oh, that didn't work

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.009
and I'm still here, that's amazing!

00:09:41.009 --> 00:09:44.880
The worst possible thing
that could happen, happened,

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.438
and nothing happened, I'm still intact,

00:09:48.438 --> 00:09:51.279
I'm still in one piece,
so my creative failures

00:09:51.279 --> 00:09:53.337
have that feeling to them where it's like,

00:09:53.337 --> 00:09:56.865
wow that totally didn't
work, and I'm totally fine,

00:09:56.865 --> 00:09:59.258
we're all going to
live, it's totally fine.

00:09:59.258 --> 00:10:02.353
Look something happened to me,

00:10:02.353 --> 00:10:05.175
but even saying that kind
of puts it in a little bit

00:10:05.175 --> 00:10:09.107
too passive of a voice, I
set out looking for something

00:10:09.107 --> 00:10:13.270
very specific and through
a pretty rigorous search

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:16.062
and a pretty rigorous set of practices

00:10:16.062 --> 00:10:19.167
I got that thing that I wanted.

00:10:19.167 --> 00:10:23.039
Which to put it really simply
and perhaps over simply,

00:10:23.039 --> 00:10:24.872
is a certain calmness.

00:10:28.721 --> 00:10:32.314
Life is an extremely
agitating event and I vibrate

00:10:32.314 --> 00:10:37.222
at a slightly higher frequency
than is necessarily healthy

00:10:37.222 --> 00:10:40.021
and I always have, I've
always been kind of anxious,

00:10:40.021 --> 00:10:42.638
I've always been super emotive,

00:10:42.638 --> 00:10:46.424
I feel things harder than is good for me,

00:10:46.424 --> 00:10:49.150
indecision has been part of my life,

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:51.753
and I could see after
a certain age how that

00:10:51.753 --> 00:10:53.355
was destructive not only to myself

00:10:53.355 --> 00:10:54.684
but to people around me.

00:10:54.684 --> 00:10:56.669
And I just think you get to a point

00:10:56.669 --> 00:10:59.003
where you don't want to
be in other peoples' way,

00:10:59.003 --> 00:11:03.258
you don't want to be taking
up other peoples' space

00:11:03.258 --> 00:11:05.642
and energies to take care of you,

00:11:05.642 --> 00:11:09.278
and I longed to be a
different kind of person.

00:11:09.278 --> 00:11:12.273
I longed to be a little more at ease,

00:11:12.273 --> 00:11:16.482
a little more relaxed,
and a little more wise,

00:11:16.482 --> 00:11:17.537
because it seems to me

00:11:17.537 --> 00:11:20.954
that wisdom is the beginning of serenity,

00:11:23.493 --> 00:11:24.815
and aside from people

00:11:24.815 --> 00:11:26.216
who are very heavily medicated,

00:11:26.216 --> 00:11:29.425
the calmest people that I know
tend to also be the wisest.

00:11:29.425 --> 00:11:34.081
Who are quickest or most
instinctively able to put things

00:11:34.081 --> 00:11:37.733
in a perspective that takes
away the urgency of the moment.

00:11:37.733 --> 00:11:40.923
And there are practices that
you can do to get there.

00:11:40.923 --> 00:11:43.718
And those practices, as my
friend Richard from Texas

00:11:43.718 --> 00:11:46.223
always says, the (bleep)
works, those practices work.

00:11:46.223 --> 00:11:48.895
And people do it in
different ways and they do it

00:11:48.895 --> 00:11:51.729
in different places, but
I think the end result

00:11:51.729 --> 00:11:53.288
and the end desire is always the same,

00:11:53.288 --> 00:11:55.970
you want peace, if not in the world,

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:58.520
then at least within your own mind.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:00.465
If you can't feel like you're alive

00:12:00.465 --> 00:12:03.055
unless you're burning bridges behind you,

00:12:03.055 --> 00:12:05.408
light the match, walk
away, that's your deal.

00:12:05.408 --> 00:12:06.241
- Fire it up.

00:12:06.241 --> 00:12:08.224
- That's not the contract
that I've ever had

00:12:08.224 --> 00:12:11.345
with my creativity because for me,

00:12:11.345 --> 00:12:13.498
we live in this sort
of bumper sticker world

00:12:13.498 --> 00:12:16.987
where the two bumper stickers
that I always want to edit,

00:12:16.987 --> 00:12:20.711
with a sharpie, in the parking lot,

00:12:20.711 --> 00:12:22.919
there's the one that says jump
and the net will catch you,

00:12:22.919 --> 00:12:24.158
and there's the one that
said what would you do

00:12:24.158 --> 00:12:26.204
if you knew you could not fail.

00:12:26.204 --> 00:12:27.899
So the edits I want to make are,

00:12:27.899 --> 00:12:29.809
jump and the net might catch you,

00:12:29.809 --> 00:12:30.642
(laughs)

00:12:30.642 --> 00:12:35.035
and what would you do even
if failure wasn't even

00:12:35.035 --> 00:12:36.588
a word that you were caring about?

00:12:36.588 --> 00:12:37.760
Which is a long edit and makes

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:38.707
for a terrible bumper sticker,

00:12:38.707 --> 00:12:41.957
but what I'm getting at is I don't like

00:12:45.183 --> 00:12:48.548
the ethic that says,
if you try really hard,

00:12:48.548 --> 00:12:50.224
and you put everything into it,

00:12:50.224 --> 00:12:51.996
you're going to get
eveerything that you want.

00:12:51.996 --> 00:12:54.021
Because we're all grown-ups here,

00:12:54.021 --> 00:12:58.341
so can we just say that
that may or may not happen.

00:12:58.341 --> 00:13:01.411
You may or may not get
everything that you want.

00:13:01.411 --> 00:13:03.599
You might put everything into something

00:13:03.599 --> 00:13:06.145
and it doesn't work and that's okay

00:13:06.145 --> 00:13:10.007
if you didn't mortgage your
house, risk your family,

00:13:10.007 --> 00:13:13.790
empty out your IRA, and put yourself

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:15.517
in a really precarious situation

00:13:15.517 --> 00:13:18.100
such that you can never do it again.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.943
That's the other thing, such
that, you've been so battered

00:13:20.943 --> 00:13:24.530
by how you set up your life
so that now you're so cornered

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:28.212
and strapped and anxious and shamed,

00:13:28.212 --> 00:13:29.045
- Yes.

00:13:29.045 --> 00:13:30.655
- that you just say, well
if that's what it feels like

00:13:30.655 --> 00:13:33.263
to give 100 percent to
something, you can keep it,

00:13:33.263 --> 00:13:34.647
I'm going to take nothing but safe choices

00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:35.944
for the rest of my life and that's it

00:13:35.944 --> 00:13:37.349
and you just shut down, right,

00:13:37.349 --> 00:13:40.054
I never want to see somebody
put in that situation.

00:13:40.054 --> 00:13:41.709
So the contract that I made with writing

00:13:41.709 --> 00:13:44.158
when I was 15 years
old, and I lit my candle

00:13:44.158 --> 00:13:46.193
and made my deal with the universe

00:13:46.193 --> 00:13:48.930
and said I'm going to be a
writer for the rest of my life,

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:51.072
one of the promises that
I made to the work was,

00:13:51.072 --> 00:13:54.242
I will never ask you to
support me financially,

00:13:54.242 --> 00:13:55.854
I will support both of us.

00:13:55.854 --> 00:13:58.654
I am a resourceful person,
my parents raised me

00:13:58.654 --> 00:14:01.289
to be a worker, I will
do whatever I have to do

00:14:01.289 --> 00:14:05.194
to pay the rent and you and
I will have a love affair

00:14:05.194 --> 00:14:08.120
on the side of this that
is not contingent upon

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:11.930
monetization, and I have
watched so many creative people

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.135
murder their creativity by insisting

00:14:14.135 --> 00:14:16.961
that they are not truly
creative unless their creativity

00:14:16.961 --> 00:14:19.418
pays the bills, and if
it doesn't pay the bills,

00:14:19.418 --> 00:14:21.577
which it might or might
not, or it might for a while

00:14:21.577 --> 00:14:23.888
and then it might not, you
might go out of fashion,

00:14:23.888 --> 00:14:25.441
your thing might change,
people might not want

00:14:25.441 --> 00:14:28.681
that anymore, and all of
a sudden, you're stuck,

00:14:28.681 --> 00:14:31.660
and I see those people go
into depression, bitterness,

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.077
rage, resentment,

00:14:35.941 --> 00:14:39.385
you have to be childlike in the pursuit

00:14:39.385 --> 00:14:42.654
of your life, but you cannot be childish.

00:14:42.654 --> 00:14:44.743
And this is a really big difference.

00:14:44.743 --> 00:14:47.240
Childlike means walking into the world

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:49.699
with wide open wonder and being open and

00:14:49.699 --> 00:14:52.632
letting go of bitterness
and ready to be amazed,

00:14:52.632 --> 00:14:54.943
ready to be taught, ready
for everything to be new.

00:14:54.943 --> 00:14:56.983
That's childlike.

00:14:56.983 --> 00:15:01.044
Childish means I want
it and I should have it.

00:15:01.044 --> 00:15:04.346
I don't like the way this
turned out, it's not fair,

00:15:04.346 --> 00:15:05.815
I'm going to have a temper tantrum now,

00:15:05.815 --> 00:15:08.123
nothing ever goes my way, I didn't grow up

00:15:08.123 --> 00:15:10.793
in the right family, I
don't have the right tools,

00:15:10.793 --> 00:15:12.381
I didn't get to go to the right school,

00:15:12.381 --> 00:15:16.548
nobody likes me, I quit,
and just because I want it

00:15:17.953 --> 00:15:20.217
I should have it, that's childish.

00:15:20.217 --> 00:15:22.150
So you have to separate those things out,

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:25.424
I believe you can be childlike
and mature at the same time.

00:15:25.424 --> 00:15:28.309
And mature means looking after
yourself in the real world,

00:15:28.309 --> 00:15:29.352
in a real way.

00:15:29.352 --> 00:15:32.591
I write in seasons, and I'll
say that if anybody out there

00:15:32.591 --> 00:15:36.953
is an aspiring writer as
everybody is and should be,

00:15:36.953 --> 00:15:39.583
and I do always encourage
aspiring writers,

00:15:39.583 --> 00:15:43.089
I would say, if you're at that level,

00:15:43.089 --> 00:15:46.038
you should be writing
everyday because nobody else

00:15:46.038 --> 00:15:48.325
will make you do it, you
have no incentive to do it,

00:15:48.325 --> 00:15:50.296
nobody's waiting for
it, if you're unknown,

00:15:50.296 --> 00:15:52.657
no editor is knocking on the
door saying there's a deadline,

00:15:52.657 --> 00:15:54.705
you have to create your own deadlines,

00:15:54.705 --> 00:15:57.013
your own motivation, your own
structure and your own time,

00:15:57.013 --> 00:15:59.741
and I did that when I was
an unpublished young writer

00:15:59.741 --> 00:16:02.542
but now at this point,
the way that I've evolved

00:16:02.542 --> 00:16:05.031
as a writer is that it's
seasonal labor for me.

00:16:05.031 --> 00:16:07.343
And there are seasons for research,

00:16:07.343 --> 00:16:09.537
and there are seasons for
publicity as we're doing

00:16:09.537 --> 00:16:11.029
right now once the book is finished,

00:16:11.029 --> 00:16:12.341
there are seasons for writing,

00:16:12.341 --> 00:16:13.762
there are seasons for editing,

00:16:13.762 --> 00:16:16.863
there are seasons to pause,
and so now it goes on a cycle,

00:16:16.863 --> 00:16:19.423
and right now I'm just beginning

00:16:19.423 --> 00:16:21.879
the season of research for the next thing.

00:16:21.879 --> 00:16:23.914
But I don't try to muddy my head by...

00:16:23.914 --> 00:16:26.927
And also it's pointless and
it's kind of a waste of time

00:16:26.927 --> 00:16:28.462
for me to write everyday if I don't have

00:16:28.462 --> 00:16:29.768
a thing to write yet.

00:16:29.768 --> 00:16:31.823
I need to go collect a lot of information

00:16:31.823 --> 00:16:33.823
before I can actually put it together

00:16:33.823 --> 00:16:35.321
and I'd rather have all that done

00:16:35.321 --> 00:16:38.156
and then sit down and do
the whole thing at once.

00:16:38.156 --> 00:16:41.810
I am inherently and naturally
an incredibly fearful person.

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:45.977
I'm operating constantly at
like 90 percent fear threshold,

00:16:47.325 --> 00:16:50.181
I'm afraid right now, I woke up afraid,

00:16:50.181 --> 00:16:52.613
I'm going to go to bed
afraid, I was born afraid,

00:16:52.613 --> 00:16:56.994
I always sort of, been
tremulous with fear,

00:16:56.994 --> 00:17:01.518
but my only saving grace
in my life is that I am

00:17:01.518 --> 00:17:04.119
one percent more curious about the world

00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:05.275
than I am afraid of it.

00:17:05.275 --> 00:17:08.243
And that's all you need,
it just has to be one gram

00:17:08.243 --> 00:17:12.382
more curiosity than fear, it's
not about eradicating fear,

00:17:12.382 --> 00:17:15.392
it's not about eliminating
fear, it's not about this,

00:17:15.392 --> 00:17:18.001
I think of a particularly American thing

00:17:18.001 --> 00:17:19.649
that's going on right now
of this idea of like kicking

00:17:19.649 --> 00:17:22.024
fear in the ass and punching
it out and showing it

00:17:22.024 --> 00:17:25.210
who's boss, this kind of
extreme sports language

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:27.499
that a lot of people
are using around fear.

00:17:27.499 --> 00:17:31.666
That doesn't work for me
because anything I ever fight

00:17:32.952 --> 00:17:35.378
in my life tends to fight back harder,

00:17:35.378 --> 00:17:37.761
so anytime I've ever tried to attack fear,

00:17:37.761 --> 00:17:39.936
it just decides to show
me how much more powerful

00:17:39.936 --> 00:17:42.304
than me it is and it just roars
to life in a much bigger way

00:17:42.304 --> 00:17:46.675
so that whole war against
fear doesn't really work.

00:17:46.675 --> 00:17:50.265
And so for me it's always been about,

00:17:50.265 --> 00:17:53.508
what do I have that's
slightly stronger than fear

00:17:53.508 --> 00:17:56.428
and for me it's just
about curiosity, wonder,

00:17:56.428 --> 00:17:59.685
inquisitiveness, I'm terrified
to see what's on the other

00:17:59.685 --> 00:18:01.908
side of that hill but I really
want to see what's on the

00:18:01.908 --> 00:18:02.987
other side of that hill.

00:18:02.987 --> 00:18:05.182
I'm terrified to see what
will happen if I try to do

00:18:05.182 --> 00:18:09.158
this project, but I'm
also slightly more curious

00:18:09.158 --> 00:18:12.253
to see what will happen
and oftentimes when my

00:18:12.253 --> 00:18:15.170
fear arises, which it does nonstop,

00:18:16.336 --> 00:18:19.091
I try to engage in a conversation with it,

00:18:19.091 --> 00:18:21.651
and so the first thing I do is thank it,

00:18:21.651 --> 00:18:23.427
which I don't think we do enough,

00:18:23.427 --> 00:18:25.267
I don't think we show enough
gratitude to our fear,

00:18:25.267 --> 00:18:27.604
all it's trying to do is keep you alive,

00:18:27.604 --> 00:18:30.743
and save your life, so I
thank it for caring about me

00:18:30.743 --> 00:18:33.323
and trying to protect me and
then I try to explain to it

00:18:33.323 --> 00:18:35.709
what I'm doing and why
we're doing this and I know

00:18:35.709 --> 00:18:38.211
that your job is to tell
me all the worst outcomes

00:18:38.211 --> 00:18:40.365
that can happen, but I
really want to do this

00:18:40.365 --> 00:18:42.221
and if it still won't back down,

00:18:42.221 --> 00:18:44.556
I usually come to my fear and I ask it

00:18:44.556 --> 00:18:47.952
for it's alternative suggestions.

00:18:47.952 --> 00:18:49.969
Okay so I want to do this project,

00:18:49.969 --> 00:18:52.574
and you, my fear, are telling me not to.

00:18:52.574 --> 00:18:56.895
So what do you have to offer
me, what's your suggestion?

00:18:56.895 --> 00:18:58.550
And when you challenge it like that,

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:01.509
the answer is always nothing.

00:19:01.509 --> 00:19:05.011
It has nothing to offer,
it never has a better idea.

00:19:05.011 --> 00:19:06.921
It has never once had a better idea.

00:19:06.921 --> 00:19:09.812
It just doesn't want me to do anything.

00:19:09.812 --> 00:19:11.025
It would just prefer that I stayed

00:19:11.025 --> 00:19:15.223
in a darkened room with a cool
damp cloth on my forehead,

00:19:15.223 --> 00:19:16.476
hiding from the world forever.

00:19:16.476 --> 00:19:19.001
And that's not going to do it for me.

00:19:19.001 --> 00:19:21.068
That's not an interesting
enough alternative.

00:19:21.068 --> 00:19:24.371
And until my fear gives me a better idea,

00:19:24.371 --> 00:19:27.389
than the cockamamie weird
jacked up idea that I have,

00:19:27.389 --> 00:19:30.106
I kind of have to do the
cockamamie weird jacked up

00:19:30.106 --> 00:19:33.507
idea because at least it's a thing.

00:19:33.507 --> 00:19:36.410
Cause the alternative
is always do something

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:38.169
even though it might not work and people

00:19:38.169 --> 00:19:40.178
might not like it and you
might not make money from it

00:19:40.178 --> 00:19:42.651
and it might be strange
and it might be a disaster,

00:19:42.651 --> 00:19:44.628
at least it's a thing.

00:19:44.628 --> 00:19:47.811
The alternative that fear
always offers is a blank

00:19:47.811 --> 00:19:50.162
piece of paper, I got nothing for ya.

00:19:50.162 --> 00:19:54.001
Just don't, just shut
it down, just nothing.

00:19:54.001 --> 00:19:55.440
And for me when the alternative

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:59.004
is so clearly between
something and nothing,

00:19:59.004 --> 00:20:02.308
I'm just not nihilistic
enough to go with nothing,

00:20:02.308 --> 00:20:05.376
I'm just curious enough to
have to go with something.

00:20:05.376 --> 00:20:08.905
Which isn't a sort of act of
faith that it's going to work.

00:20:08.905 --> 00:20:11.575
I had a letter recently
from a woman who said,

00:20:11.575 --> 00:20:13.535
I think I'm about ready
to start writing my book

00:20:13.535 --> 00:20:16.555
but I don't want to
start until I'm convinced

00:20:16.555 --> 00:20:19.527
that the universe will give
me the outcome that I want.

00:20:19.527 --> 00:20:20.469
(laughs)

00:20:20.469 --> 00:20:24.875
I was like, yeah that is
never going to happen.

00:20:24.875 --> 00:20:26.715
The universe might not
give you the outcome

00:20:26.715 --> 00:20:28.216
that you want, we're not children here.

00:20:28.216 --> 00:20:32.374
Like we all know, jump and
the net might not catch you,

00:20:32.374 --> 00:20:33.267
(laughs)

00:20:33.267 --> 00:20:35.504
but the universe will give you an outcome,

00:20:35.504 --> 00:20:36.833
it's going to give you something,

00:20:36.833 --> 00:20:37.992
you're going to know more about yourself

00:20:37.992 --> 00:20:39.479
than you did at the beginning of the thing

00:20:39.479 --> 00:20:41.488
certainly, it might
not be what you wanted,

00:20:41.488 --> 00:20:42.998
it might not be what you expected,

00:20:42.998 --> 00:20:45.434
but it'll be something, and always,

00:20:45.434 --> 00:20:47.328
always, always given the
choice between something

00:20:47.328 --> 00:20:51.495
and nothing, I'm going to
have to choose something.

00:20:53.212 --> 00:20:54.355
- Thank you so much for watching,

00:20:54.355 --> 00:20:57.008
I made this video because
Phoebe asked me to,

00:20:57.008 --> 00:20:58.929
so if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:20:58.929 --> 00:21:01.083
want me to profile next,
leave it in the comments

00:21:01.083 --> 00:21:02.679
below and I'll see what I can do.

00:21:02.679 --> 00:21:04.273
I'd also love to know which of Elizabeth's

00:21:04.273 --> 00:21:06.457
top 10 rules had the
biggest impact on you,

00:21:06.457 --> 00:21:09.025
what lesson most stuck out for you,

00:21:09.025 --> 00:21:11.695
leave it in the comments
below and I will join

00:21:11.695 --> 00:21:12.630
in the discussion.

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:15.230
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe,

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:17.063
and I'll see you soon.

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:25.075
Oh, I'm great at boundaries.

00:21:25.075 --> 00:21:27.027
I'm not great at boundaries, I'm kidding.

00:21:27.027 --> 00:21:29.010
I so thought that was
going to be a laugh line,

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:31.025
I thought you guys knew me,

00:21:31.025 --> 00:21:34.182
I thought you knew me well
enough to know that, what?

00:21:34.182 --> 00:21:36.159
No, I don't have any boundaries,

00:21:36.159 --> 00:21:39.576
I'm the permeable membrane, I don't know.

00:21:41.382 --> 00:21:45.132
I'm a little better at
it, but I have learned

00:21:46.249 --> 00:21:49.865
one important thing which is that you

00:21:49.865 --> 00:21:53.211
sort of get to a point in
your life where you think,

00:21:53.211 --> 00:21:55.873
I have to learn how to say no
to things I don't want to do,

00:21:55.873 --> 00:21:57.721
and that's a big mature moment when you

00:21:57.721 --> 00:22:00.913
start to begin to do that,
but the bigger mature moment

00:22:00.913 --> 00:22:02.049
is when you learn how to say no

00:22:02.049 --> 00:22:03.618
to things you do want to do.

00:22:03.618 --> 00:22:06.805
Because you know that
you just don't have time

00:22:06.805 --> 00:22:10.939
or you don't have energy or
you have to sort of triage

00:22:10.939 --> 00:22:13.759
your life and there just
isn't an opportunity.

00:22:13.759 --> 00:22:15.529
And that only came to me very recently

00:22:15.529 --> 00:22:18.114
that sometimes you have to
turn down even the wonderful

00:22:18.114 --> 00:22:19.960
things in order to take
care of your health,

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:23.627
or to take care of what
you truly, truly, truly

00:22:23.627 --> 00:22:26.264
care about, not just what
you truly care about.

00:22:26.264 --> 00:22:30.269
No catharsis ever happened
without somebody's participation.

00:22:30.269 --> 00:22:31.913
People talk about cathartic experiences,

00:22:31.913 --> 00:22:34.385
somebody has a horrible accident
or a horrible heartbreak

00:22:34.385 --> 00:22:38.825
and it transforms them but
not without their assistance.

00:22:38.825 --> 00:22:40.898
Not without them showing up for it.

00:22:40.898 --> 00:22:44.219
And I think travel can always
seem like such a natural

00:22:44.219 --> 00:22:48.201
way for people to change their
lives but it can't impose

00:22:48.201 --> 00:22:51.406
change on you, you can only will it in.

00:22:51.406 --> 00:22:54.989
And similarly, whatever
beautiful treasures

00:22:55.958 --> 00:22:58.084
of selfhood that I found on this journey

00:22:58.084 --> 00:23:00.534
are mine to lose any day I feel like it.

00:23:00.534 --> 00:23:03.743
And so I consider it my obligation to hold

00:23:03.743 --> 00:23:06.037
onto as much of that
as I can within reason.

00:23:06.037 --> 00:23:08.969
I would so love to tell
you that I get up everyday

00:23:08.969 --> 00:23:11.761
before dawn and meditate
and I'm half tempted

00:23:11.761 --> 00:23:13.850
to tell you that because
how the hell would

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:15.644
you guys ever know if it's true or not

00:23:15.644 --> 00:23:16.910
but in fact it's not true,

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:18.418
(laughs)

00:23:18.418 --> 00:23:21.202
but I do it more days
than I don't, and I think

00:23:21.202 --> 00:23:25.130
that for that I kind of base
it on my writing practice

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:27.382
as well, I would love to
say that I write everyday

00:23:27.382 --> 00:23:29.155
but I don't, but I write
more days than I don't.

00:23:29.155 --> 00:23:32.133
And I think as long as in
your life you're doing more

00:23:32.133 --> 00:23:35.413
of what's beneficial to you
than not, then that's probably

00:23:35.413 --> 00:23:38.829
good enough for what we
can do in our normal lives.

00:23:38.829 --> 00:23:40.515
You can only make this decision

00:23:40.515 --> 00:23:42.714
based on what you know today.

00:23:42.714 --> 00:23:47.052
And so whatever you decide
to do, about this pregnancy,

00:23:47.052 --> 00:23:49.361
about this person that
you're involved with,

00:23:49.361 --> 00:23:51.746
about this situation that you're in,

00:23:51.746 --> 00:23:54.169
all you know is what you know right now.

00:23:54.169 --> 00:23:57.202
And the decision's
going to have to be made

00:23:57.202 --> 00:23:59.440
based on that, so you are going to have

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:03.607
to pre-forgive yourself for
what you don't know yet.

00:24:05.518 --> 00:24:09.374
And it may be that years
later, when you have

00:24:09.374 --> 00:24:12.038
more information about your
life and about the world

00:24:12.038 --> 00:24:14.282
you may look back on the
decision you made this day,

00:24:14.282 --> 00:24:17.392
whatever it is, and you may
wish you had made a different

00:24:17.392 --> 00:24:19.932
decision, but you have to begin right now,

00:24:19.932 --> 00:24:23.694
forgiving yourself for
what you can't know yet.

00:24:23.694 --> 00:24:26.244
I feel like that is the most compassionate

00:24:26.244 --> 00:24:29.226
possible advice that anybody
could be given when they're

00:24:29.226 --> 00:24:31.856
making a difficult decision in your life.

00:24:31.856 --> 00:24:34.851
And then later, to go back
and look, cause we spend

00:24:34.851 --> 00:24:37.155
so much time mired in regret,

00:24:37.155 --> 00:24:39.362
about things we should have known,

00:24:39.362 --> 00:24:40.578
things we should have done,

00:24:40.578 --> 00:24:42.746
things we shouldn't have done,

00:24:42.746 --> 00:24:45.031
and at some point there's this mercy

00:24:45.031 --> 00:24:47.307
that you have to show
to yourself that says,

00:24:47.307 --> 00:24:50.408
every decision I've ever made in my life

00:24:50.408 --> 00:24:53.075
I made based on what I knew then

00:24:54.797 --> 00:24:57.206
and that's all I had to work with.

00:24:57.206 --> 00:25:01.188
You can't know something
before you know it,

00:25:01.188 --> 00:25:02.515
it's just a rule of life,

00:25:02.515 --> 00:25:05.385
and I think about some of the
stuff I've done in my life

00:25:05.385 --> 00:25:08.552
and how stupid it was, and I look back

00:25:09.565 --> 00:25:11.173
on a younger version of myself and think,

00:25:11.173 --> 00:25:14.121
how could you have not known better,

00:25:14.121 --> 00:25:18.079
and the only compassionate
and merciful response is,

00:25:18.079 --> 00:25:19.762
because I didn't yet.

00:25:19.762 --> 00:25:23.288
And maybe I had to do
that terrible stupid thing

00:25:23.288 --> 00:25:25.539
in order to learn, so
that I could become a more

00:25:25.539 --> 00:25:27.032
mature person who wouldn't do it again.

00:25:27.032 --> 00:25:28.536
Maybe I had to do it 10 times!

00:25:28.536 --> 00:25:30.392
Maybe I had to do it for 15 years.

00:25:30.392 --> 00:25:32.480
Maybe I had to do it for 30 years.

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:36.018
But you're not going to learn
something until you learn it.

00:25:36.018 --> 00:25:39.244
And then at that moment you
must, immediately, forgive

00:25:39.244 --> 00:25:42.624
younger versions of yourself
for what she didn't know yet.

00:25:42.624 --> 00:25:46.032
And you must, in advance, I
have to sort of look ahead

00:25:46.032 --> 00:25:49.590
and know that when I'm
56, I'm going to look back

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:51.999
at myself now at 46 and be like,

00:25:51.999 --> 00:25:54.332
how could you not have seen,

00:25:55.454 --> 00:25:58.716
you can't see what you don't see yet.

00:25:58.716 --> 00:26:01.374
I think what we're most
missing in our lives

00:26:01.374 --> 00:26:05.036
both in the public level
and the private level

00:26:05.036 --> 00:26:08.021
and in the personal level is mercy.

00:26:08.021 --> 00:26:09.634
Just this sense of mercy.

00:26:09.634 --> 00:26:13.894
We're all doing the best
we can with what we know

00:26:13.894 --> 00:26:16.455
at any given moment and we
simply can not do better

00:26:16.455 --> 00:26:18.690
than that, I can't, you
can't, your family can't,

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:21.417
the world can't, it's all we've got.

00:26:21.417 --> 00:26:26.039
So be a little gentler with
yourself and with others

00:26:26.039 --> 00:26:27.846
and with their mistakes as well.

